---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import {
  GlassBannerPlayground,
  GlassBannerStarters,
  GlassBannerLayouts,
  GlassBannerIntegrations,
} from "../../components/GlassBannerDemo";
---

<Docs
  title="Glass Banner"
  description="A frosted showcase banner with glass chips you can drag and fling."
>
  <h1 class="docs-title">Glass Banner</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    A soft blue glass panel for a page's opening moment. A title and caption sit
    on bubble art, and a few glass chips show what happens next. Drag a chip and
    let go: it stretches with its speed and springs home.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8"><GlassBannerPlayground client:load /></div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Glass banner usage"
      code={`import { BrandIcon } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/brand-icon";
import { GlassBanner, GlassChip } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/glass-banner";

<GlassBanner title="Connect once. Your agents work there." caption="Example activity">
  <GlassChip icon={<BrandIcon brand="gmail" size="glyph" />}>Drafted 3 follow-ups</GlassChip>
  <GlassChip icon={<BrandIcon brand="slack" size="glyph" />}>Answered in #support</GlassChip>
  <GlassChip icon={<BrandIcon brand="jira" size="glyph" />}>Triaged 5 new issues</GlassChip>
</GlassBanner>`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>GlassBanner</code> takes a <code>title</code>, an optional <code
        >caption</code
      >, and <code>GlassChip</code> children. Each chip takes text and an optional
      <code>icon</code>, drawn in a 28px white bubble. Brand glyphs and 16px
      icons both fit. The banner has one size: at least 240px tall with 24px
      padding. Light and dark modes each have their own glass colors.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Icons and text chips</h2>
    <p>
      Chips with an icon pad tightly around the bubble; text-only chips get even
      padding on both ends. This set mixes both.
    </p>
    <GlassBannerStarters client:load />
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Layout</h2>
    <p>
      The banner reads its own width, not the window. From 704px wide, up to
      three chips float over the art at fixed spots, each tilted a little.
      Narrower banners, or banners with four or more chips, wrap the chips in
      rows under the title. Long chip text wraps instead of overflowing.
    </p>
    <GlassBannerLayouts client:load />
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Integrations page</h2>
    <p>
      The integrations page opens with the banner when the <b>All</b> filter is on
      and the search is empty. Searching or picking another filter hides it so results
      come first. Try it below.
    </p>
    <GlassBannerIntegrations client:load />
    <p>
      Apps, filters, and activity here are sample data. The package only draws
      the banner and its chips.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      The bubble art is <code>aria-hidden</code>. Chips are a list named by the
      caption, or by the title when there is no caption. Dragging is pointer
      play only: chips are not controls, so they take no focus and keyboard
      users read them as plain list items. Chips float gently and spring back
      after a drag; with reduced motion they hold still and snap home as soon as
      you let go.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
